{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkX4F5fAhKmrB8zGPMZx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MoyzbeinRgX1gyT7Y2MF11","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Clef, two sharps. A rest, marked \"pianj.\", is followed by low slurred notes with 16th strokes and then a single high flagged 8th with a stroke. This is bar 0, the pickup: D4, (D4 E4 D4), then D5 staccato. Barline. Next comes a group of eight 8ths in two slurred, beamed fours. This is bar 1: (D5 C#5) C#5 E5 (E5 Bb4) Bb4 A4. Barline. Next is a slurred pair, a note written low with a mark below it, then four notes rising under a long slur. This is bar 2: (A4 G4) G3 G4, then (G4 Bb4 C#5 E5). Barline. Next are eight 8ths alternating with a high repeated note, marked \"pia.\" above. This is bar 3: G5 alternating with C#5, Bb4, A4 and G4. Barline. Last is a slurred pair and then a quarter note, with \"fortis.\" above and below. This is the first half of bar 4: (G4 F4) F4 quarter. The ff second half (D5+D4, E5 trill…) is missing. The \"//\" at the right edge and the \"fortis\" cue point to the next line. So this line ends partway through bar 4. Nothing contradicts the edition.","right":true,"insights":"The barlines are clear and each bar's outline is distinctive, which made this easy. The pickup, bar 2's rising four-note run and bar 3's alternating 8ths are good examples. The hard part was the line's last bar: it holds only half of bar 4. The \"fortis\" just before the line break, which is the edition's ff, confirms where the split falls. Exact pitches were hard to read on the faint staff lines, so more contrast in the image would help."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5432,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5432},"output_tokens":5,"se